+# Symlinked project roots
+
+`list`, `test`, and `clean` accept a project path that is a symbolic link. This
+page defines what that means. It is the behavior from v0.1.x, now covered by
+tests and made observable; there is no compatibility change.
+
+## Resolution
+
+The project path is resolved once, to a canonical directory (all symlinks
+followed), before anything else happens. Everything afterwards uses the
+resolved root:
+
+| Area | Behavior |
+| --- | --- |
+| Discovery | Walks the real directory. Symlinks found *inside* the project are not followed, as before. |
+| Reporting | File paths in text, JSON, Markdown, HTML, and SARIF reports are relative to the resolved root. The symlink's name never appears in them. |
+| Ordering, IDs, shards | Identical to invoking the tool on the real directory, because they depend only on project-relative paths. |
+| Caches and scratch | `.soro-mutants-target` and `.soro-mutants-worktree` are created inside the resolved root. A relative `--target-dir` is also joined to the resolved root. |
+| Result cache and resume identity | Built from the resolved root, so a run through the link and a run through the real path share a cache. |
+
+For identical source, configuration, and toolchain, a run through a symlink and
+a run through the real directory produce identical reports.
+
+## Cleanup safety
+
+`clean` removes only Soro Mutants-managed paths under the resolved root. The
+symlink you passed is never removed, and nothing outside the real project is
+touched. If a managed path inside the project, such as `.soro-mutants-target`,
+is itself a symlink, `clean` refuses with
+`refusing to clean symlinked Soro Mutants path ...` and exits 1 without deleting
+anything, so a link cannot redirect deletion to another directory.
+
+## Diagnostics
+
+When the project path itself is a symlink, stderr gets one line:
+
+```text
+note: project path ./contracts-link is a symlink; using resolved root /work/contracts for discovery, reports, and cleanup
+```
+
+The note is not printed for ordinary directories, and stdout (including
+`--json` and `--output` reports) is unchanged. A symlink in an *ancestor*
+directory is resolved silently.
+
+## Errors
+
+Failures exit 1 with an `error: cannot resolve <path>` line, never a panic:
+
+| Condition | Message |
+| --- | --- |
+| Target of the link no longer exists | `error: cannot resolve ./link: symlink points to /old/place, which does not exist; fix or recreate the link, or pass the real project directory` |
+| Link cycle | `error: cannot resolve ./a: too many levels of symbolic links; the project path is part of a symlink cycle` |
+| Any other failure | `error: cannot resolve ./path: <operating-system error>` (unchanged from earlier releases) |
+
+## Platforms
+
+Symlink behavior is tested on Unix. On Windows, creating symlinks needs
+developer mode or elevated rights; the resolution code path is the same, but the
